+## 6h. Task 13: why the input dither gains less in the wave model than in the ray model
+
+`scripts/t13_wave_vs_ray.js` → `results/t13/` (`table.md`; sweeps `ray_vs_wave`, `amplitude`, `lambda`, `regular`). Circle cell,
+set B, R 97 %, 0.3 dB/cm, input dither only, 15 chaotic layouts and the regular cell; the Task 9 beamlet model and, on the same
+layouts, the engine's own angle-dither factor V_θ (the ray model behind the Fig. 2f bars).
+
+Cause. The cell is linear: the exit field is U E_in, and the stray copy that it beats with does not follow the input state.
+The static fringe of any input dither is therefore the fringe of the time-averaged input field, F = s† U ⟨E_in⟩. The
+dither never changes the optical path of any route through the cell (only the wavelength does); it only changes how
+strongly each route is excited. For a mixing cell (s† U a random vector) |F|/|F₀| = |⟨E_in⟩|/|E_in| = N^(−½), N the number
+of orthogonal input states swept: a property of the input beam's phase space (waist, tilt, shift, λ), not of the cell.
+Chaos is needed to reach this bound, and cannot go below it. The ray model launches one zero-width ray per dither angle and
+follows its exit path, whose length changes by many wavelengths per mrad (FTLE 0.53 per bounce): it treats every tilt as a
+new route with a new optical path, i.e. a wavelength-like phase sweep of a single route, which a beam cannot produce.
+
+Findings.
+1. The wave factor follows N^(−½) within ±30 % in every configuration: angle, position, combined, sine and triangle,
+   N = 1–740, and at scaled wavelengths (Table). The ray factor is ≈ 1e-2 at every amplitude (±1 to ±200 mrad) and
+   every waist: it does not depend on the beam at all, the signature of the single-ray artefact.
+2. Latest design (75 µm, ±60 mrad, ±100 µm, sine): N = 403, wave factor 0.058 (17×), the 6.8e-6 → 5.9e-7 of Fig. 2 (j). The input
+   dither does help, by the N^(−½) the beam's phase space allows; the ray model's ≈ 1e-2 (100×) at ±2 mrad corresponds to
+   N ≈ 10⁴, beyond any beam of this size.
+3. Angle alone: N ≈ 0.25 κ A w grows linearly with the tilt (75 µm: N 3.5 → 28 for ±15 → ±200 mrad, factor 0.55 → 0.20);
+   position alone sweeps almost nothing (±200 µm: N 2.9). Combined they multiply: ±100 mrad and ±100 µm with a triangle
+   waveform gives N = 743 and factor 0.039 (1.5× below the current design); the sine waveform dwells at the turning points
+   and gives N = 369 there.
+4. Geometric-optics limit: shrinking λ 100× at fixed geometry raises N ∝ 1/λ (40 µm, ±2 mrad: 1.0 → 16.7) and the wave factor
+   falls as N^(−½) (0.98 → 0.18); the ray factor falls as well (1.5e-2 → 1.5e-3). The ray model is not the short-wavelength limit
+   of the beam: at every λ it counts ≈ 100× more independent states than the beam can sweep.
+5. Regular cell: the beamlet-model numbers change 4× with σ (undithered 0.15–0.66), so they remain indicative only; the
+   chaotic factors agree within ±30 % for σ = 7–14 µm.
+
+Consequences. The input dither is bounded by the swept phase space, not by chaos: more states need a larger waist × tilt ×
+shift (triangle waveforms), a deeper wavelength dither, or a modulation inside the cell (local heaters on inclusions or a
+facet). The last changes U itself, i.e. the optical paths, so its gain is not bounded by N; there chaos does matter (each
+setting gives a new speckle realisation, up to M ≈ 300).
+
